      {
        "id": 210906,
        "name": "The Burning Stickman",
        "username": "The_Burning_Stickman",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/the_burning_stickman/{size}/2003_2.png",
        "created_at": "2017-12-15T03:57:40.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"Trajector\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/trajector/48/1914_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Trajector:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I was really surprised by how well the Argent Adept did. Just by how his deck handles…I figured he can barely do anything by himself, and he exists to make other heroes better. I would not have expected him to stand up in any fights, and I figured the Naturalist would have a relatively straightforward apprentice-beats-master victory.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Well, this is Prime Wardens AA – he sets up at double the speed and can really come out swinging. Argent Adept&nbsp;<em>usually</em> just supports others, but he has consistent damage in his deck and can buff himself considerably. All he'd really have to do is keep neutralizing Naturalist until he's ready to pull out all the instruments and chain together a dozen instances of damage, each of them buffed.</p>",
